WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.090
Film Courage: What is the stupidest way to
write a screenplay?

00:00:04.090 --> 00:00:07.120
Mark Sanderson, Screenwriter/Author: Well
you are talking to someone who is a big fan

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:12.349
of the outline so…and not that the person
who doesn’t is stupid…

00:00:12.349 --> 00:00:14.420
Film Courage: But those are my words not yours…

00:00:14.420 --> 00:00:15.420
Mark: Your words…

00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:17.660
Film Courage: My words…Karen’s words.

00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:19.900
Mark: Send the letters and cards.

00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:21.610
Film Courage: They know the email.

00:00:21.610 --> 00:00:25.740
Mark: It would just to sit down and say “I
got an idea.

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:29.000
A half-baked idea that really wasn’t thought
out.”

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:33.040
And you can do what you want which is fine
but I just think you know and you may find

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:37.700
there’s a scene in there that is great and
brilliant but a cohesive through line and

00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:42.680
all that it takes time, it takes work.

00:00:42.680 --> 00:00:46.260
This isn’t something that just spills out.

00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:48.570
Some people say I can just spill a script
out in a week.

00:00:48.570 --> 00:00:52.970
Well that’s just fantastic and maybe you
can do that.

00:00:52.970 --> 00:01:01.420
Everybody has their own way but there are
a lot of pitfalls in doing that with the story

00:01:01.420 --> 00:01:06.119
and things aren’t working and I hate when
I sit there and I write something that I know

00:01:06.119 --> 00:01:15.020
is just bad and you know we all go through
that but the best times are where it’s like

00:01:15.020 --> 00:01:20.829
this weird channeling and I can see it in
my mind’s eye and that’s going onto the

00:01:20.829 --> 00:01:27.439
page and I feel good about it because I already
watched it in the theater but it hasn’t

00:01:27.439 --> 00:01:33.890
existed yet in my head so in a way when it’s
really good it feels like you’re channeling

00:01:33.890 --> 00:01:34.890
it.

00:01:34.890 --> 00:01:40.659
When it’s bad there is something that the
channel, those waves are not coming through

00:01:40.659 --> 00:01:41.899
not to be like Oohhh!

00:01:41.899 --> 00:01:44.740
Film Courage: You’re on dial-up versus…

00:01:44.740 --> 00:01:51.659
Mark: Sure you’re tapping into your creative
thing, your well whatever you want to call

00:01:51.659 --> 00:01:57.909
it where you go and it’s…beep…the signal
is not and you just write down a scene and

00:01:57.909 --> 00:02:01.060
it’s like “That’s going to have to be
re-written.”

00:02:01.060 --> 00:02:06.659
Imagine…now a person probably wouldn’t
think that just burning through a script “Oh

00:02:06.659 --> 00:02:08.450
this is great.”

00:02:08.450 --> 00:02:12.090
But it’s just bad when you’re like “Oh
my gosh, it’s garbage.”

00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:16.290
It’s terrible like a placeholder instead
of the real scene.

00:02:16.290 --> 00:02:21.250
Yeah okay well this is just the bad version
as everyone says.

00:02:21.250 --> 00:02:27.420
You get that note from people well here is
the bad version and they tell you it and you’re

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:31.170
like that’s really not that bad but no,
no, no I don’t want you to do that.

00:02:31.170 --> 00:02:36.290
Here is an example of what not to do you know
the bad version is as you hear in Hollywood

00:02:36.290 --> 00:02:39.530
a lot and you’re like “Are you ever going
to tell me the good version?”

00:02:39.530 --> 00:02:41.860
And they say “No, that’s for you to figure
out.”

00:02:41.860 --> 00:02:44.080
I’m like “Oh, you’re right!

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:46.569
You’re right because I am the writer.

00:02:46.569 --> 00:02:48.319
Throw me a lifeline here.

00:02:48.319 --> 00:02:52.829
I’m drowning because I’ve got the bad
version that’s the real bad version.”

00:02:52.829 --> 00:02:55.409
Film Courage: Is this SUNSET BOULEVARD?

00:02:55.409 --> 00:02:56.859
Mark: Exactly.

00:02:56.859 --> 00:03:01.260
Film Courage: Is it almost similar to the
people that in college said “I don’t have

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:03.840
to study, I just show up for tests?”

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:06.430
Mark: In a way and there are some that can.

00:03:06.430 --> 00:03:16.159
I’ve never met any but yes especially I
think with the odds.

00:03:16.159 --> 00:03:22.640
There are so many scripts out there and so
many good scripts too.

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:29.930
That I think a lot of times writers may not
see the mountain that they’re climbing everyday.

00:03:29.930 --> 00:03:35.870
You are not only up against your own competing
with yourself to be a better writer next time

00:03:35.870 --> 00:03:41.400
and next time learn like you should but also
what is actually out there if you want to

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:42.860
work in the marketplace type of thing.

00:03:42.860 --> 00:03:52.659
And I know when you say that, when I say that
it sounds very non-romantic but it’s a business

00:03:52.659 --> 00:03:59.501
so why not put your best foot forward in creating
that unless you have a good day job and there

00:03:59.501 --> 00:04:05.930
is no pressure that this script has to sell
to make your rent or your mortgage payment

00:04:05.930 --> 00:04:11.280
and you are just like Yeah, I’ll dabble
in it but dabbling takes time as well as so

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:18.970
while you are dabbling another person is working
24 hours a day learning the craft and really

00:04:18.970 --> 00:04:28.840
passionately they want it more than anything
else and it can be a hobby it’s fine but

00:04:28.840 --> 00:04:34.810
I think there are ways to approach a career
or a writing career in a certain way and people

00:04:34.810 --> 00:04:43.220
can try however they want but back to your
original question it’s just to go I think

00:04:43.220 --> 00:04:48.500
I have an original idea and not really Whoah…hold
on a second.

00:04:48.500 --> 00:04:51.229
What are you doing here with this idea?

00:04:51.229 --> 00:04:58.740
Every idea is a kernel of a premise that builds,
but do we have enough type of thing?

00:04:58.740 --> 00:05:00.500
And not to second guess what is selling.

00:05:00.500 --> 00:05:06.740
If it’s really a passionate idea you want
to write absolutely you must do it.

00:05:06.740 --> 00:05:15.371
Film Courage: And what about people who say
that I don’t want to write for TV or I don’t

00:05:15.371 --> 00:05:20.410
want to write a movie that doesn’t have
any A-list talent in it and they stomp on

00:05:20.410 --> 00:05:27.760
something because it’s not STAR WARS?

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:29.240
What do you say to that?

00:05:29.240 --> 00:05:34.240
They want to be a screenwriter, they want
to work in the industry but oh…no that’s

00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:42.400
not 48 HOURS or sorry let me pick something
from the last few decades.

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:44.720
Mark: I know what you mean.

00:05:44.720 --> 00:05:48.729
You’re saying they stomp on the smaller
ideas?

00:05:48.729 --> 00:06:00.009
Yes, I read a lot of scripts that are big,
big ideas (Hollywood ideas) which is fantastic

00:06:00.009 --> 00:06:06.419
but the real odds of the unknown, uncredited
writer it could happen.

00:06:06.419 --> 00:06:07.539
It does once in awhile.

00:06:07.539 --> 00:06:10.490
Not as much as it used to in the past.

00:06:10.490 --> 00:06:16.530
Selling that thing that’s the only thing
they’ve written because intellectual property

00:06:16.530 --> 00:06:18.400
(IP) is all the rage.

00:06:18.400 --> 00:06:24.800
It’s already been a book, a comic book that’s
been a sequel or a prequel.

00:06:24.800 --> 00:06:26.629
STAR WARS is a perfect example.

00:06:26.629 --> 00:06:30.840
We just manufacture until the end and then
we come up with a new series, right?

00:06:30.840 --> 00:06:35.330
That’s all done and then we veer off on
this one and we’ll take that character and

00:06:35.330 --> 00:06:37.290
make a whole series on that one.

00:06:37.290 --> 00:06:45.259
That’s a bigger…you’re trying to hit
a home run out of the park which we all do

00:06:45.259 --> 00:06:51.650
all of the time but I think you’re also
shrinking your possibilities because they

00:06:51.650 --> 00:06:55.940
already have the A-list writers out filling
those jobs.

00:06:55.940 --> 00:06:56.940
How do they get there?

00:06:56.940 --> 00:07:00.530
I don’t know, everybody has their journey
if you trace.

00:07:00.530 --> 00:07:08.370
I was saying a writer who was a big TV person
now in features started on XENA PRINCESS WARRIOR.

00:07:08.370 --> 00:07:13.189
Now people would look down and be…Xena…that’s
a ridiculous blah, blah, blah.

00:07:13.189 --> 00:07:19.169
Everybody starts somewhere and was it his
heart’s desire to work on that show forever,

00:07:19.169 --> 00:07:20.169
no?

00:07:20.169 --> 00:07:21.710
But that was a stepping ground.

00:07:21.710 --> 00:07:24.879
Everybody has that stepping ground.

00:07:24.879 --> 00:07:30.790
My experience and the people I’ve known
you don’t just start out at the top.

00:07:30.790 --> 00:07:36.300
You take the job and you go yeah okay it’s
not my perfect thing but I now I will…it’s

00:07:36.300 --> 00:07:39.599
a step-by-step process.

00:07:39.599 --> 00:07:45.439
I would say in that way don’t step on those
little ideas because those are the ones that

00:07:45.439 --> 00:07:46.889
can get made.

00:07:46.889 --> 00:07:51.069
You’re taking a bigger gamble writing this
hundred million dollar script that’s locked

00:07:51.069 --> 00:07:52.069
into that budget.

00:07:52.069 --> 00:07:56.379
It can’t be made for less so then you say
so where can it be made.

00:07:56.379 --> 00:07:59.840
I don’t know there’s four studios that
can possibly do this.

00:07:59.840 --> 00:08:02.719
Let’s say they all pass where are you going
to go with the script?

00:08:02.719 --> 00:08:07.610
Somebody is going to have to shell out a 100
million dollars of their own money.

00:08:07.610 --> 00:08:15.181
So the smaller idea (smaller in scope) meaning
we’re not out to save the universe (the

00:08:15.181 --> 00:08:22.240
universe could be your own life) your little
universe could actually be made for five million

00:08:22.240 --> 00:08:23.699
dollars, two million dollars.

00:08:23.699 --> 00:08:27.189
It’s not glamorous.

00:08:27.189 --> 00:08:30.570
Like a lot of writers “Well I’ll just
sell my script for millions of dollars.”

00:08:30.570 --> 00:08:32.760
And you think your life changes overnight.

00:08:32.760 --> 00:08:37.130
You still have the same problems, you still
have the same relationship, you’re still

00:08:37.130 --> 00:08:38.130
a person.

00:08:38.130 --> 00:08:40.660
It’s just a job.

00:08:40.660 --> 00:08:45.690
I hate to sort of shrink it down to that “Oh
a screenwriter!”

00:08:45.690 --> 00:08:48.503
I used to think the same way too.

00:08:48.503 --> 00:08:49.540
Oh my gosh, you read the trades you’re like
“Oh!”

00:08:49.540 --> 00:08:52.770
I’m going to live the life.

00:08:52.770 --> 00:08:53.770
Live the life, yeah.

00:08:53.770 --> 00:08:56.850
You’re going to be living the life at 2
in the morning writing the screenplay.

00:08:56.850 --> 00:08:58.200
That’s what it is, it’s work.

00:08:58.200 --> 00:08:59.200
It’s not glamour.

00:08:59.200 --> 00:09:04.530
There are times when you’re forgotten about
at a screening.

00:09:04.530 --> 00:09:09.710
It happened where they thank everybody and
they thanked the craft service person and

00:09:09.710 --> 00:09:11.440
they go “Let’s run the film.”

00:09:11.440 --> 00:09:13.880
And you’re sitting with the stars and they
look at you and you’re mortified.

00:09:13.880 --> 00:09:18.230
You’re like [emits nervous laughter].

00:09:18.230 --> 00:09:24.310
Then afterwards of course it was just an oversight
and at the party it was like Oh my gosh, so

00:09:24.310 --> 00:09:25.310
sorry.

00:09:25.310 --> 00:09:26.310
Have another martini…

00:09:26.310 --> 00:09:27.310
Film Courage: I’ll call you.

00:09:27.310 --> 00:09:32.430
Mark: It wasn’t that bad but you know what
I mean, it’s not all going to be and if

00:09:32.430 --> 00:09:41.270
you’re in it for that fame and fortune and
fanfare not the writer I mean we made inroads

00:09:41.270 --> 00:09:48.460
a little more than the past but you’re still
the writer.

00:09:48.460 --> 00:09:55.150
It’s one of the most important people because
that’s where without a script there is nothing

00:09:55.150 --> 00:10:01.660
right but that’s why you should try to be
a hyphenate writer/producer, writer/director

00:10:01.660 --> 00:10:08.770
or something like that eventually or start
out as that’s your choice, that’s your

00:10:08.770 --> 00:10:14.360
career direction but just the writer it’s
an interesting road.

00:10:14.360 --> 00:10:15.270
Question For The Viewers: How was that helpful
to you?

